speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I believe any hon. members or any others in Canada who think the oil exploration in the northern Arctic islands will be the type of thing which will lend itself to speculation underestimates the judgment of the people of Canada. First of all I would like to make it clear that we have not issued any exploratory permits, as this article states, with regard to the Arctic islands and the continental shelf. What we are issuing at the present time are exploratory licences valid for one year which give the applicant the right only to carry out general geological reconnaissance. The exploratory permits, if and when issued to permittees, will give them the right to carry out all types of exploratory work, such as seismic, of geophysical and geochemical, which includes drilling, and also the right to obtain up to 50 per cent of the grid area as an oil and gas lease. It is in my opinion likely that speculation taken in anticipation of any rights which may he acquired upon obtaining exploratory permits will be minimized because section 10 of the territorial oil and gas regulations provides that the crown must be satisfied that the applicant is able to undertake exploratory work of value. The government intends that each applicant shall furnish the following information:
(a)	Proof of financial ability to carry out the necessary exploratory work on the area applied for;
(b)	The latest audited financial statement of the company or the individual;
(c)	The exploration program the applicant intends to carry out during the first half of the term of the exploration permit.
Mr. Speaker, I might conclude by saying that the author of the Maclean's magazine article referred to is not only out of touch with our oil and gas regulations but is also out of touch with government policy. I can assure the house that the public interest is being safeguarded to the maximum within our powers.

Mr. Chairman, in connection with clause 4, I presume you will be calling the schedule separately, because I have an amendment. I may say that the change that I wish to make will affect the reference in line 23 to clause (d) of section 4 of the bill. I would ask my colleague, the Minister of National Revenue, to move the following amendment, that Bill No. C-44 be amended as follows:
(1)	By deleting the numbers "438d, 438e" where they appear in clause 4 on line 23 of page 2;
(2)	By striking out the reference to tariff item "411a" where it appears on page 16 in tariff item 438b in part IV of the schedule;
(3)	By striking out the reference to tariff item "411a" where it appears on pages 17 and 18 in tariff item 438c in part 4 of the schedule;
(4)	By deleting from part IV of the schedule tariff items 438d and 438e on pages 18. 19 and 20.

Item agreed to.
Customs tariff-156. (a) Whiskey, per gallon o£ the strength of proof and In addition thereto, under all tariffs, $9.00 per gallon of the strength of proof: British preferential tariff, $4.50; most favoured nation, $5.00; general tariff, $10.00.
(b)	Gin, n.o.p., per gallon of the strength of proof and in addition thereto, under all tariffs, $9.00 per gallon of the strength of proof: British preferential tariff, $4.50; most favoured nation, $5.00; general tariff, $10.00.
(c)	Rum, n.o.p., per gallon of the strength of proof and in addition thereto, under all tariffs, $9.00 per gallon of the strength of proof: British preferential tariff, $4.50; most favoured nation, $6.00; general tariff, $10.00.
(d)	Brandy, per gallon of the strength of proof and in addition thereto, under all tariffs, $9.00 per gallon of the strength of proof: British preferential tariff, $4.00; most favoured nation, $4.00; general tariff, $10.00.
(e)	Liqueurs, per gallon of the strength of proof and in addition thereto, under all tariffs, $9.00 per gallon of the strength of proof: British preferential tariff, $4.50; most favoured nation, $4.50; general tariff, $10.00.
(f)	Ethyl alcohol, or the substance commonly known as alcohol, hydrated oxide of ethyl or spirits of wine, n.o.p.; spirituous or alcoholic liquors, n.o.p.; absinthe, arrack or palm spirit, artificial brandy and imitations of brandy, n.o.p.; cordials of all kinds, n.o.p. mescal, pulque, rum shrub, schiedam and other schnapps; tafia, and alcoholic bitters or beverages, n.o.p.; and wines, n.o.p., containing more than forty per cent of proof spirit,

per gallon of the strength of proof and in addition thereto, under all tariffs, $9.00 per gallon of the strength of proof: British preferential tariff, $5.00; most favoured nation, $10.00; general tariff, $10.00.
When the goods specified in item 156 are of greater or less strength than the strength of proof, the measurement thereof and the amount of duty payable thereon shall be increased or decreased in proportion for any greater or less strength than the strength of proof.
Bottles and flasks and packages of gin, rum, whiskey and brandy of all kinds, and imitations thereof, shall be held to contain the following quantities (subject to the provisions for addition or deduction in respect of the degree of strength), namely:
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing not more than one-eight of a gallon per dozen as one-eighth of a gallon per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than one-eighth of a gallon but not more than one-sixth of a gallon per dozen, as one-sixth of a gallon per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than one-sixth of a gallon but not more than one-fourth of a gallon per dozen, as one-fourth of a gallon per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than one-fourth of a gallon but not more than one-half of a gallon per dozen, as one-half of a gallon per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than one-half of a gallon but not more than three-fourths of a gallon per dozen, as three-fourths of a gallon per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than three-fourths of a gallon but not more than one gallon per dozen, as one gallon per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than one gallon but not more than one and one-half gallons per dozen, as one and one-half gallons per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than one and one-half gallons but not more than two gallons per dozen, as two gallons per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than two gallons but not more than two and four-fifths gallons per dozen, as two and four-fifths gallons per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than two and four-fifths gallons but not more than three gallons per dozen, as three gallons per dozen;
Bottles, flasks and packages, containing more than three gallons but not more than three and one-fifth gallons per dozen, as three and one-fifth gallons per dozen.
Bottles or phials of liquors for special purposes, such as samples not for sale to the trade, may be entered for duty according to actual measurement, under regulations prescribed by the minister.

It is deleted by the amendment. Item No. 438e is also deleted by the amendment.
Items 438f, 440c, 440k, 446e, 462d, 462i, 479, 502, 502c, 505a. 611a, agreed to.

Customs Tariff
Customs tariff-682:
Fish hooks, for deep-sea or lake fishing, not smaller in size than number 2.0;
Fishing nets and nettings of all kinds;
Lures, jiggers and artificial baits;
Metal panel devices for use in keeping nets open;
Metal swivels, of a class or kind not made in Canada;
Net and line floats of any material except wood;
Specially designed needles of a class or kind not made in Canada for use in repairing fish nets;
Threads, twines, marlines, fishing lines, rope and cordage, not exceeding one and one-half inches in circumference;
All the foregoing for use in commercial fishing, under such regulations as the minister may prescribe;
Carapace measures of any material: British preferential tariff, free; most-favoured-nation tariff, free; general tariff, free.
